.TH pipemixer 1 "March 2026" "@VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pipemixer \- audio mixer for pipewire

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pipemixer
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B pipemixer
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGUMENTS\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
pipemixer is a terminal-based audio mixer for the pipewire audio system.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-config
Path to configuration file, see \fBpipemixer.ini\fR(5) for details.
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-loglevel
Set log level. Accepted values: DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR, QUIET.
.TP
.B \-L, \-\-log-fd
Write log output to the specified file descriptor (must be open for writing).
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-color
Force colored log output.
.TP
.B \-j, \-\-json
Print JSON for query commands.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-timeout \fIMS\fR
Set the command deadline in milliseconds (default: 5000).
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Display help message and exit.

.SH NONINTERACTIVE COMMANDS
Options must appear before the command. A target is an exact PipeWire
.B node.name
or
.B device.name,
.B id:N,
or, for nodes,
.B serial:N.
Use
.B list
to discover names and IDs. A channel is a short channel name such as
.B FL
or
.B FR.
.TP
.B list [nodes|devices|ports|links|graph|buses|sends|effects]
List audio nodes (the default), audio devices, audio ports, actual audio links,
or the complete audio graph. With --json, graph returns an object containing
nodes, ports, and links arrays. Monitor ports and filter nodes are included;
MIDI/video ports and internal peak-meter streams are excluded.
The buses, sends and effects lists show one input node per managed path.
.TP
.B diagnostics [MILLISECONDS]
Sample professional monitoring for 100..60000 ms (default 1000). Report system
and process CPU, audio-process CPU relative to one core, RSS, available RAM,
PipeWire node state, actual quantum/rate, wait/processing time, DSP load, xrun
counts and recent errors. Unavailable profiler measurements are explicit.
.TP
.B meter TARGET [MILLISECONDS]
Measure per-channel sample peak, RMS, peak hold, clipped samples (absolute value
at least 1) and invalid samples. Duration uses the same bounds as diagnostics.
These commands extend the default timeout to measurement duration plus 5000 ms.
Meters do not calculate LUFS, intersample true peak or frequency spectra.
.TP
.B start-history NAME SECONDS NODE...
Start a detached bounded audio cache: 1..8 unique sources, 1..120 seconds,
64 MiB per-session memory budget including capture queues, 64 MiB available-RAM
admission reserve. Names follow the managed-name convention. Source IDs/serials
resolve to stable names. Capture uses stereo 48 kHz float PCM and a common
monotonic frame timeline with 100 ms guard. Missing sources fill silence and
reconnect by name; independent devices do not share a hardware word clock.
.TP
.B list-history
List active caches in this config-directory scope, with recording state and
resource counters. Internal capture streams are excluded from ordinary graphs.
.TP
.B history-status NAME
Report cache fill, CPU/RSS, tracks, missing sources, queue drops, gaps, recording
state, output directory, elapsed frames/bytes, free disk space and last error.
.TP
.B export-history NAME NEW_DIRECTORY [SECONDS]
Export recent audio as one stereo float WAV per track and session.json metadata.
SECONDS defaults to the full cache; only available history is exported. Stop
continuous recording first; exporting from an actively recording cache is rejected
to avoid delaying its file writer. Each
track has the same frame range. The parent must exist; the destination must be
new and existing files are never replaced. Default operation timeout: 30000 ms.
.TP
.B record-history NAME NEW_DIRECTORY [PREROLL_SECONDS]
Start continuous multitrack recording from an existing cache. Optional preroll
is 0..the configured cache duration (default zero). Produces per-source float
WAVs in 60-second parts and atomic session.json metadata. Headers and metadata
are checkpointed once per second. The file writer runs outside audio callbacks.
.TP
.B stop-recording NAME
Finalize files while keeping the cache running. Repeated stops succeed.
.TP
.B stop-history NAME
Finalize an active recording and release its cache. Sessions survive TUI exit
but are not restored after reboot. SIGINT/SIGTERM also finalize active WAVs.
.PP
Recording reserves at least 16 MiB disk space; shortage stops recording while
keeping the cache. Set PIPEMIXER_RECORD_RESERVE_MB=16..1024 and
PIPEMIXER_RECORD_SEGMENT_SECONDS=1..3600 before creating the cache to change
these limits. One stereo track writes 384000 bytes/s. TUI output defaults to
$XDG_STATE_HOME/pipemixer/recordings or ~/.local/state/pipemixer/recordings;
on small boards choose mounted storage. /tmp may be a RAM filesystem.
SIGKILL/power loss may lose the latest checkpoint interval. Metadata reports
frame ranges, sources, gaps, capture drops and segment details.
.TP
.B get-volume TARGET [CHANNEL]
Print channel volumes in percent. With CHANNEL, print one numeric value.
.TP
.B set-volume TARGET PERCENT [CHANNEL]
Set all channels or one channel to an absolute volume from 0 to 150 percent.
.TP
.B get-mute TARGET
Print on or off.
.TP
.B set-mute TARGET on|off|toggle
Set or toggle mute.
.TP
.B get-default sink|source
Print the name of the current default audio sink or source.
.TP
.B set-default TARGET
Set an audio sink or source as the default.
.TP
.B list-routes TARGET
List the routes of a node with their indices and active status.
.TP
.B create-monitor NAME DESTINATION_NODE [SOURCE_NODE...]
Save an independent stereo monitor. The supervised routing-daemon creates its
audio path and connects the normal mix to the chosen destination. Names contain
1-32 letters, digits, hyphens or underscores; node names must be stable names.
Definitions support up to 32 monitors and 32 sources each, use atomic files
with mode 0600, and reload after audio-server restart or reboot.
.TP
.B set-monitor-sources NAME NODE...|off
Replace the normal monitor mix, or empty it. Solo and listening choice remain.
.TP
.B set-monitor-source NAME NODE|mix
Listen to one source or the normal mix. Active Solo takes precedence; clearing
Solo returns to the stored listening choice.
.TP
.B solo-monitor NAME NODE on|off|toggle
Add or remove a Solo source. Multiple Solo sources mix together on this monitor;
source volume/mute and main output/recording connections are retained.
.TP
.B clear-monitor-solo NAME
Clear all Solo and return to the previous listening source or normal mix.
.TP
.B enable-monitor NAME on|off
Enable or disable managed monitor routes without changing the sources.
.TP
.B delete-monitor NAME
Delete the persistent definition and its own monitor path.
.TP
.B check-monitors
Validate persistent monitor JSON without connecting to PipeWire. Monitor edits
also work offline. Use the board audio service to apply definitions.
.TP
.B list-monitors
Query live monitor state. JSON includes normal/active sources, Solo, listening
source, mode, connection counts and engine status. During switching, old or
incorrectly owned feeds prevent a connected report. Missing selected sources
stay silent. Foreign configurations or unmanaged monitor input links report
conflict and suspend the managed output. Closing the TUI keeps monitoring active.
The path is pipemixer.monitor.NAME.input/.output; use regular volume/mute commands
on its output for an independent listening level. Scenes capture path, gains
and link ownership; persistent monitor policy supplies the current mix/Solo.
.TP
.B set-route TARGET INDEX
Activate a listed route.
.TP
.B set-target STREAM DESTINATION|default
Set the sink for a playback stream or the source for a recording stream.
Use default to resume following the system default device.
.TP
.B list-profiles DEVICE
List the profiles of an audio device with their indices and active status.
.TP
.B set-profile DEVICE INDEX
Activate a listed profile.
.TP
.B connect OUTPUT_PORT INPUT_PORT
Connect two audio ports, using exact node.name:port.name values or id:N.
The first port must be an output and the second an input. Existing connections
are left in place. New connections that would form a feedback loop are rejected.
.TP
.B disconnect OUTPUT_PORT INPUT_PORT
Remove all links between two audio ports. An already disconnected pair succeeds.
.TP
.B create-bus NAME
Create a stereo virtual sink and source named pipemixer.bus.NAME.input and
pipemixer.bus.NAME.output. Applications can play into the sink and record the
mix from the source.
.TP
.B delete-bus NAME
Remove a managed bus. Removing an already absent bus succeeds.
.TP
.B create-send NAME SOURCE_NODE DESTINATION_NODE
Copy audio from a node with output ports to a node with input ports, without
changing its original path. The send has independent volume/mute on
pipemixer.send.NAME.output. Creation waits for its actual links. Fixed targets
do not fall back to another device. Feedback loops are rejected.
.TP
.B delete-send NAME
Remove a managed send. Removing an already absent send succeeds.
.TP
.B create-effect NAME eq|voice|empty|@CONFIG_FILE
Create a stereo filter chain with a virtual sink/source pair named
pipemixer.effect.NAME.input and pipemixer.effect.NAME.output. eq provides a
three-band equalizer; voice provides a high-pass filter, noise gate and
presence EQ. Custom files supply a filter.graph object (maximum 1 MiB).
empty creates a pass-through chain with no processors.
Other stream properties in the file are ignored. Plugins must be installed.
.TP
.B delete-effect NAME
Remove a managed effect. Removing an already absent effect succeeds.
.TP
.B effect-params NAME
List DSP parameter names, current values and ranges. JSON also includes the
default and writable flag. Frequencies use Hz, EQ gains dB, Q is dimensionless,
noise-gate thresholds linear amplitudes, and time parameters seconds.
.TP
.B set-effect-param NAME PARAMETER VALUE
Apply a finite value within the listed range without recreating the chain.
.TP
.B bypass-effect NAME on|off
Switch between processed and original audio. Custom graphs need wet:Mult and
dry:Mult controls with the same semantics as the built-in presets.
.TP
.B effect-chain NAME
List processor IDs, types, order and individual bypass states.
.TP
.B add-effect-stage NAME TYPE [POSITION]
Insert a processor, by default at the end. Positions start at 1; the limit is
16 processors. Types: lowshelf, peaking, highshelf, highpass, lowpass, gain,
gate, compressor, limiter. Compressor and limiter require pipemixer-dynamics.so.
.TP
.B remove-effect-stage NAME STAGE
Remove the processor with that ID and its parameters.
.TP
.B move-effect-stage NAME STAGE POSITION
Move a processor to the specified position. Structure edits briefly rebuild
the chain and restore its current state, connections and dependent Sends.
The default structure-edit deadline is 30 seconds.
.TP
.B bypass-effect-stage NAME STAGE on|off
Bypass one processor. After converting a fixed preset to an editable chain,
bypass and parameter changes do not replace the audio nodes.
Compressor controls use dB, milliseconds and a dimensionless ratio; limiter
Ceiling dB limits digital samples at that processor's output, without lookahead
or intersample true-peak detection. Stereo detectors are independent.
.TP
.B save-scene NAME
Atomically save the audio graph, managed path definitions, DSP parameters,
channel volumes, mute, stream destinations and defaults using stable names.
Version 2 also saves hardware device/profile names and per-node hardware routes.
.TP
.B load-scene NAME
Restore a scene, recreating missing managed paths and reusing matching ones.
External nodes must be present. Extra links between saved nodes are removed;
unlisted managed paths are retained. The default timeout is 30 seconds.
Runtime failure removes newly created paths; existing state may need reloading.
.TP
.B list-scenes
List saved scene names, optionally as JSON. Does not require PipeWire.
.TP
.B check-scene NAME
Validate scene format, dependencies and feedback without applying it.
Does not require PipeWire.
.TP
.B delete-scene NAME
Delete a scene file. An absent scene succeeds. Does not alter running audio.
Deleting the selected startup scene disables startup restoration.
.TP
.B set-startup-scene NAME|off
Validate and select a saved scene for startup restoration. The special value
off disables restoration. Selection is atomic and does not require PipeWire.
.TP
.B get-startup-scene
Print the startup scene name, or off when disabled. Accepts JSON output and
does not require PipeWire.
.TP
.B restore-startup
Restore the selected startup scene. Disabled restoration succeeds without
PipeWire. Absent devices/application streams and dependent sends are skipped
and reported; independent paths are restored. Other conflicts still fail.
The default timeout is 30 seconds. Saved scene files are not modified.
.PP
Scenes are stored under $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pipemixer/scenes, falling back to
$HOME/.config/pipemixer/scenes. Scene names use the managed name rules.
Custom effect graphs are embedded. Version 2 restores hardware profiles, waits
for their nodes, selects hardware routes, then restores parameters and links.
Version 1 remains readable without hardware mode changes. Startup restoration
skips absent/unavailable hardware and dependent paths; late supported hardware
is restored by the routing engine. Unknown profile/route names report blocked
in recovery-status. Temporary unavailability waits for capability changes.
New snapshots retain automatic link ownership. Restored tagged connections
can still be removed by their rule engine; older untagged snapshots restore
manual connections.
Startup selection is stored in the sibling startup-scene file. Restoration
runs when invoked; integrate it after PipeWire and the session manager start.
The supplied Luckfox SysV installer provides a persistent root audio service
at /etc/init.d/S98pipemixer. Devices appearing after startup can reconnect
through routing rules and selective scene replay. Successful load-scene or
restore-startup activates an audio-session snapshot. The routing engine
replays channel gains, mute, targets, defaults, dependent sends and manual
links for new object generations, preserving live changes on existing nodes.
Current rule/monitor policy owns tagged links. Initialization has bounded
retries; persistent conflicts wait for an explicit scene reload.
.TP
.B recovery-status
Show the active recovery scene and per-node waiting/restoring/restored/blocked
states, plus hardware profile, route and stream-target progress. Accepts JSON. Restored means this generation has completed replay;
subsequent live adjustments, including manual hardware choices, are preserved.
.TP
.B clear-recovery
Disable audio-session scene replay without altering audio, startup selection
or routing/monitor policy. Does not require PipeWire. Engine restarts retain
the private runtime ledger; loading a scene activates a fresh snapshot.
.TP
.B create-route-rule NAME OUTPUT_PORT INPUT_PORT [OPTIONS]
Save an enabled rule using node.name:port.name endpoints. Endpoints can be
absent. Session IDs are rejected. Existing names cannot be overwritten.
Matching defaults to exact, with a separate rule for each stereo channel.
Use --match glob for quoted shell patterns (*, ?, brackets, backslash escapes).
Matching ports connect only to the same audio.channel; unknown channels require
a single port on each side. No implicit mono/stereo conversion is performed.
Limits are 256 matches per side, 1024 pairs per rule and 8192 pairs per rule set.
Use --priority N (-1000000..1000000) and --exclusive-group NAME to choose one
rule per source node. Higher priority wins; ties use ascending rule names.
Candidates must cover all matching source channels. Ungrouped rules retain
fan-out; manual connections and other rule sets retain their ownership.
Repeat --fallback INPUT_PORT for up to eight ordered backup targets using the
same match mode. Each source selects the first viable candidate and switches
its channels together. Missing, blocked or failed targets allow the next
candidate. A better candidate must remain viable for --switch-delay MS
(0..60000, default 1000) before failback. Negotiation failures have a five-second
cooldown. Switching removes owned old links before creating replacements.
.TP
.B set-route-priority NAME N [GROUP|off]
Edit priority and optionally the exclusive group. Omission keeps the group;
off clears it. Works offline and retains other settings.
.TP
.B set-route-fallbacks NAME INPUT...|off [--switch-delay MS]
Replace the ordered backup list, or clear it with off. A delay-only edit
retains the list. Duplicate selectors, including the primary, are rejected.
Works offline and retains other settings.
.TP
.B enable-route-rule NAME on|off
Enable or disable a saved rule. The running engine reloads changes within
one second. Disabled rules remove only their own tagged connections.
.TP
.B delete-route-rule NAME
Delete a saved rule. Missing rules succeed. The engine removes its tagged
links when it reloads. Existing manual or other rule-set links are retained.
.TP
.B check-route-rules
Validate all routing rule files without applying them. Works without PipeWire.
.TP
.B list-route-rules
Query current connection states and whether the engine is running. JSON
returns an object containing engine_running and a rules array. Each rule has
its name, enabled flag, endpoints, state and reason. States are waiting,
connecting, connected, partial, suppressed, disabled, ambiguous, blocked
(feedback), or error. JSON includes matched/selected/connected pair counts,
priority, exclusive group, suppressed pairs, fallback list, delay, using_fallback
and active_input. The last is null when no target is selected or sources use
different targets; list links shows individual endpoints.
.TP
.B routing-daemon
Run the automatic routing engine in the foreground until interrupted or
PipeWire disconnects. One engine is allowed per config directory. The board
audio supervisor runs and restarts it automatically after scene restoration.
Stopping it retains existing connections.
.PP
Rules are versioned JSON under $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pipemixer/rules, falling back
to $HOME/.config/pipemixer/rules. Files use mode 0600 and atomic replacement.
Version 1 exact rules remain supported. Advanced settings use version 2.
Creation, enabling, deletion and validation work without PipeWire. Failed
reloads retain the last valid rules. Missing endpoints wait for their exact
names to reappear, and then reconnect regardless of new object IDs. Ambiguous
names or feedback loops prevent creation. The engine repairs disconnected
enabled pairs and changes only port connections. It waits during scene loading,
then resumes the global rules shared by scenes in the same config directory.
The board engine log is /tmp/pipemixer-routing.log.
.PP
Managed names contain 1-48 ASCII letters, digits, hyphens or underscores.
Detached audio workers keep paths alive after the TUI/CLI exits, until deletion
or PipeWire shutdown. Errors are logged to
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pipemixer-KIND-NAME.log. Buses and effects do not automatically
connect to physical devices. Sends and effects need a running session manager.
.PP
Query commands accept
.B --json.
Mutation commands produce no output on success and wait for the requested
state to be reported by PipeWire. CLI commands do not require a config file
unless one is supplied with
.B --config.
.PP
Exit status: 0 for success, 1 for a PipeWire error or timeout, 2 for invalid
arguments, and 3 for an unavailable target, destination, or state.

.SH CONTROLS
By default, pipemixer uses the following keybinds:
.TP
.B j, Down arrow
Move focus to the next item.
.TP
.B k, Up arrow
Move focus to the previous item.
.TP
.B g, G
Move to the first or to the last item, respectively.
.TP
.B l, Right arrow
Increase volume.
.TP
.B h, Left arrow
Decrease volume.
.TP
.B t, Tab
Switch to the next tab.
.TP
.B T, Shift+Tab
Switch to the previous tab.
.TP
.B 1, 2, 3, 4, 5
Switch to the 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th, or 5th tab.
.TP
.B m
Toggle mute status.
.TP
.B Space
Toggle channel lock (affects whether volume changes apply to all channels).
.TP
.B p
Show device port selection menu.
.TP
.B c
Select the output device for a playback stream or the input device for a recording stream.
The menu can also restore following the system default.
.TP
.B P
Show profile selection menu.
.TP
.B D
Set focused source or sink as default.
.TP
.B r
Open or close the live audio routing matrix. Use arrows or hjkl to select
output/input ports, then Enter or Space to toggle their connection. A mouse
click on a matrix cell also toggles it. Escape returns to the mixer.
.TP
.B v, z, Z, f, /
In the matrix, cycle flat/node/type grouping with v; fold output/input groups
with z/Z. Enter/Space on a header folds it. Cycle type filters with f; / edits
case-insensitive search, Enter applies, Escape restores it, Ctrl-U clears it.
These controls change the display while connections keep running.
.TP
.B x, X, n, N, c, d, u
Mark output/input ports or groups with x/X, or all channels of their node with
n/N. c connects matching selected channels; d disconnects them; u clears marks.
Known channels pair by label. The complete plan is checked for feedback before
starting. Connection errors or Escape cancellation roll back only new links;
pre-existing links remain. Disconnect cancellation can leave partial results.
Automatic rules may reconnect links. Changing the search/type filter clears
selection; missing/replaced ports lose marks. Shift-click headers marks them.
.TP
.B M, S, L, U
M opens monitor creation/selection, normal mix/Solo editing, listening source,
enable/disable and deletion. In the matrix, creation uses the selected source
and preselects the destination. S toggles Solo for the focused source on the
selected monitor; L listens to that source; U clears all Solo. Clearing Solo
restores the previous listening choice or normal mix. Enter toggles source
membership, g/G jumps to first/last menu item and Escape closes the menu.
.TP
.B i
Open diagnostics for the focused node or matrix source. Enter resets meter
counters and recent events; Escape closes. Server xrun totals are retained.
.TP
.B R
Manage audio history and multitrack recording. Toggle source membership with
Enter/Space, then start a 10/30-second cache. Export recent/all cached audio,
start/stop continuous recording or stop the cache. Displays CPU, memory, disk
space, source availability and drops. Completed sessions survive closing TUI.
.TP
.B b
Create a stereo bus, create an independent send from the focused audio node,
or delete a managed audio path.
.TP
.B e
Create or edit effects. On a focused effect, open its ordered processor list.
Use j/k to select, Enter to edit parameters, a to add, d to remove with
confirmation, K/J to reorder, Space to bypass one processor and B for the whole
chain. On the parameter page h/l or Left/Right adjust, Enter resets and Esc
returns to the chain. Custom graphs retain their flat parameter editor.
.TP
.B s
Save the current setup as a new scene, load/delete a saved scene, select its
startup restoration or disable restoration. The header shows the selection.
New scenes use the first available sceneN name. Move with j/k and confirm
with Enter.
.TP
.B a
In the routing matrix, save the selected pair as an automatic routing rule
using the first free routeN name. From the mixer, open the rule menu to query
live states, pair counts, priority, group and fallback status; enable/disable
or delete rules with j/k and Enter. Use h/l or Left/Right to adjust priority
by 10. Group and fallback edits use the CLI.
.TP
.B A
In the routing matrix, save a batch rule for all compatible channels of the
selected source and target nodes. Literal node names are escaped before
appending a port wildcard. New matching channels join automatically.
.TP
.B Enter
Confirm selection.
.TP
.B Escape
Cancel selection or quit pipemixer if not in menu.
.TP
.B q
Quit the application.

.SH AUTOMATION AND EXTERNAL CONTROL
Press
.B o
for the automation menu. Select a node first to edit its volume fade, or an
effect node to choose a continuous parameter. Use h/l to adjust the target,
duration and curve; select Run fade to execute. The menu also enables rules,
runs their actions, cancels fades and displays MIDI/OSC status.
.PP
.B fade-volume TARGET PERCENT MS [linear|smooth]
and
.B fade-effect-param NAME PARAMETER VALUE MS [linear|smooth]
start detached fades. Durations are 20..3600000 ms. Volume fades preserve channel
balance and interpolate actual gain; parameter fades interpolate continuous
numeric controls. Manual changes or disappearance/replacement of the target stop
the fade. CLI/TUI manual edits drain the worker's earlier writes before setting
the manual value. Updates share a maximum of approximately 50 writes/second;
one fade normally updates every 20 ms, eight concurrent fades every 160 ms.
.PP
.B cancel-fade [TARGET [PARAMETER]]
cancels running fades and preserves their current values.
.B automation-status
prints live job/rule status; --json includes connection state and errors.
.PP
.B import-automation FILE
validates and atomically saves version 1 pipemixer.automation JSON in
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pipemixer/automation.json, or ~/.config/pipemixer/automation.json.
.B check-automation [FILE]
validates a file or the installed definitions.
.B enable-automation NAME on|off
persists a rule's enabled state.
.B trigger-automation NAME
runs its actions manually, bypassing conditions while respecting enable,
priority and cooldown.
.PP
.B start-automation
starts the engine and saves its supervisor preference as on.
.B stop-automation
stops it and saves off.
.B automation-daemon
is the foreground engine. The board S99pipemixer-automation service restarts
enabled engines after failures and waits for startup scene restoration/routing.
Definitions and preferences survive restart; fade progress and counters reset.
.PP
Conditions include all/any/not, presence, mute, volume, effect parameters, RMS
level, links, defaults and elapsed time. Rules provide hold/release, hysteresis,
cooldown, priorities and sequential actions; independent sequences run together.
Actions include fades, parameter/mute updates, waits, scenes, route-rule enable
and monitoring source/Solo changes. Periodic triggers use monotonic time.
.PP
OSC UDP is disabled without an osc configuration; the default configured bind is
127.0.0.1. Direct controls require direct:true. Methods are /pipemixer/volume,
/pipemixer/mute, /pipemixer/parameter, /pipemixer/scene and /pipemixer/rule.
Configured paths can map normalized input to rule actions. Replies use
/pipemixer/reply with string address, integer result and string error.
Immediate bundles are supported; future timetags and wildcard addresses are not.
.PP
MIDI input uses the configured RawMIDI character device and retries after
hotplug. Mapping types are cc, note and program, channels 1..16 and numbers
0..127. Running status, interleaved realtime bytes and velocity-zero note-off
are supported; SysEx is skipped. MIDI 2.0 and Sequencer routing are not included.
.PP
Limits: 32 rules, 256 condition nodes, 8 nesting levels, 8 actions per sequence,
16 pending sequences, 32 fades, 256 KiB configuration, and 200 OSC packets/sec.
OSC packets are limited to 4096 bytes and 16 messages. Invalid reloads retain the
last valid definitions and expose the error in status.
.PP
See docs/AUTOMATION.md and assets/automation/example.json for the complete schema,
message arguments and examples.

.SH MOUSE
Mouse input is enabled by default and can be disabled with
.B mouse=false
in the configuration file. Click a tab to switch views or a row to focus it.
Click a stream title to select its destination, a device port or card profile
row to open its menu, the volume bar to set volume, or the volume number to
toggle mute. Click a menu choice to apply it; click outside to cancel. The wheel
scrolls lists and menus. Right-click a stream to select its destination or an
output/input device to make it the default. Middle-click a node to toggle mute.

.SH BUGS
Please report bugs to https://github.com/heather7283/pipemixer/issues
.PP
When reporting bugs:
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
Provide backtrace with symbols if you experience a crash.
.IP \(bu 4
Describe steps necessary to reproduce the issue.
.IP \(bu 4
Attach output of pw-dump (note that it might contain sensitive information).
.PD

.SH SEE ALSO
pulsemixer: https://github.com/GeorgeFilipkin/pulsemixer
.br
pwvucontrol: https://github.com/saivert/pwvucontrol
.PP
.BR pipemixer.ini (5),
.BR wpctl (1)
